## Project context

### context/product.md

# Product context — Project Runtime

Project Runtime is a control plane for AI-assisted software teams. A team defines
its project's context, rules, skills, and tool access once, in a portable
`project.yaml`. The tool compiles that definition into the native configuration
of every AI tool the team uses, checks pull requests against the team's rules,
and keeps project context fresh.

It is deliberately not another AI workspace: developers keep working in their
editor or terminal. Project Runtime sits underneath and makes every one of those
tools behave consistently.

## Positioning

- Tagline: write your project's AI setup once. Sync it to every tool. Enforce it.
- Beachhead: software agencies and consultancies (5+ concurrent client projects,
  rotating developers). Second wave: startups.
- Wedge: vendor-neutral (one definition across tools), enforcement instead of
  suggestion, and later the credential/permission layer (managed MCP gateway).

## MVP scope (PRD v0.2 §8)

In: CLI (`init`, `sync`, `check`, `join`), three target adapters, GitHub App
running static and LLM checks on PRs, minimal dashboard, `project.yaml` spec v1.

Out (for the MVP): chat workspace, workflow engine, agents, skill marketplace,
gateway (beta follows MVP), SSO, self-hosting, cloud IDE.

## This repository dogfoods the product

The root `project.yaml` describes this repo itself; `runtime sync` generates
tool configs from it and `runtime check` enforces its rules on the codebase.
If the product cannot keep its own house in order, that is a bug — report it,
do not work around it.

### context/architecture.md

# Architecture context

pnpm workspace, TypeScript ESM throughout, Node >= 20.

- `packages/core` — the engine. No CLI concerns, no fs in the hot paths:
  - `schema/` — `project.yaml` spec v1 as Zod schemas (`runtime: 1` literal,
    kebab-case ids, safe repo paths, forward-slash globs).
  - `parse/` — loads `project.yaml` + referenced context/rules files
    (BOM-stripped, LF-normalized; missing files are load errors).
  - `adapters/` — pure `TargetAdapter` implementations (generate definition →
    file bytes). Registered in `registry.ts`, one line per target. Only
    `claude-code` (root `CLAUDE.md`) exists today; cursor and copilot are
    pending (M7, docs-gated).
  - `checks/` — static pattern checks (picomatch path scoping, per-line regex)
    and the shared result types. LLM-judged checks are schema + skipped
    semantics only until the judge exists (M6).
  - `drift/` — generated-file states (`unchanged`, `stale`, `hand-edited`,
    `untracked`, `new`) against `.runtime/manifest.json` (stable JSON, sorted
    keys, trailing newline).
  - `secrets/` — hand-rolled credential-shape scanning; findings are always
    redacted; `runtime:allow-secret` line marker and `secrets.ignore` provide
    allowlisting.
- `packages/cli` — commander wiring (`runtime sync`, `runtime check`) and
  command implementations that inject stdout/stderr (no direct console in
  library code).
- `apps/github-app` — stub only; the App is out of scope for now.
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — test matrix on ubuntu + windows.
- `.github/workflows/release.yml` — Node SEA single-executable builds
  (linux-x64, macOS arm64/x64 ad-hoc signed, windows-x64) + SHA256SUMS.txt.

## Exit codes (D6)

0 success; 1 changes or drift detected (`sync --verify`) or a block-severity
check failure (`check`); 2 configuration error; 3 secret detected (findings
redacted).

## Invariants worth defending

- Adapters are pure: same `CompiledProject` in, same bytes out (the drift
  manifest depends on it).
- Generated files carry the `GENERATED BY RUNTIME — DO NOT EDIT` header.
- A skipped check is never a pass; budget exhaustion and unparseable judge
  output yield `skipped` with a reason.
- All hashing uses BOM-stripped, LF-normalized content.


## Rules

### rules/coding.md

# Coding rules

## TypeScript

- Strict mode, ESM (`"type": "module"`), Node >= 20. Import other workspace
  files with the `.js` extension (tsc NodeNext).
- No new dependency without asking first (name, size, why). Zero-dependency
  implementations are preferred where the stdlib suffices.
- Library and command code never write to `console` — commands receive
  `stdout`/`stderr` as injected parameters. The single allowed exception is the
  top-level catch in `packages/cli/src/main.ts`.
- Pure functions where the design allows: adapters and static checks must stay
  free of fs, git, network, and clock access.

## Tests

- Every adapter, parser, and command gets tests in the owning package's
  `test/` directory (vitest). A bug fix lands with a failing regression test
  first, a root-cause fix, and a guard against the whole class of bug.
- Fixture files are committed with LF endings and no BOM.

## Files and formatting

- LF line endings, UTF-8 without BOM. Files generated by `runtime sync` are
  pinned to LF in `.gitattributes` — never hand-edit them.
- Check ids, project identifiers, and rule slugs are kebab-case.

## Compatibility

- Windows is a first-class platform: no POSIX-only assumptions in source,
  forward-slash globs in config, 8.3-safe repo-root detection.

### rules/process.md

# Process rules

## Evidence

- "Done" requires evidence: a file path plus a passing test name or pasted
  command output. No evidence means not done.
- Never invent external facts (tool config formats, API behavior). Implement
  only what official documentation supports; otherwise mark the work as blocked
  and say what is needed to unblock it.

## Workflow

- One milestone at a time; small commits with conventional prefixes.
  After each milestone: typecheck, tests, audit update, checkpoint, then stop
  and wait for the word to continue.
- Stop and report instead of retrying when a command exceeds five minutes, the
  same failure repeats, credentials are missing, or an external format is
  uncertain.
- No force pushes. The repository stays private. Outward-facing actions are
  confirmed first.

## Bugs

Every found bug follows the same loop: failing regression test first, root-cause
fix (not a symptom patch), a guard that prevents the class of bug from
recurring, and an entry in `docs/BUGS.md` with cause, fix, and test name.

## Secrets

Credentials come from the environment, never from files, and are never logged.
Secret findings are always redacted in any output path, including error
messages and verbose logs.
